# Building Alfredpi Choreographies

## Overview

Build timed rabbit performances using Alfredpi’s actual choreography DSL. Never invent actions or UI features; inspect the running rabbit and repository when uncertain.

## Discovery

Check the live rabbit first:

- Editor: `http://alfredpi.local/choregraphy`
- New choreography: `http://alfredpi.local/choregraphy/new`
- Swagger: `http://alfredpi.local/swaggerui`
- OpenAPI: `http://alfredpi.local/api/openapi`
- Choreographies: `http://alfredpi.local/api/choregraphies`

When repository access is available, inspect:

- `rabbit_manager/src/alfredpi_choregraphy_parser_lexer.xrl`
- `rabbit_manager/src/alfredpi_choregraphy.yrl`
- `rabbit_manager/lib/alfredpi/executor.ex`
- `rabbit_manager/priv/choregraphy/builtins.chor`

## DSL Quick Reference

Values:

- String: `"hello.mp3"`
- Integer: `250`, `-5`, `1_000`
- Color: `~RRGGBB`
- Variable: capitalized identifier such as `Delay`
- Comment: `# explanation`

Built-in variables:

- Ears: `Left`, `Right`, `Both`
- LEDs: `Led0`, `Led1`, `Led2`, `Led3`, `Led4`

Actions:

```text
move_ear Left, 5
set_ear Right, 12
wait_ear Left
led_color ~ff0000, Led2
play "https://example.com/sounds", "success.mp3"
sleep 250
```

Assignments:

```text
var Delay = 250
var Color = ~ff006e

led_color Color, Led0
sleep Delay
```

Repetition:

```text
repeat 5
  led_color ~ff0000, Led1
  sleep 200
  led_color ~000000, Led1
  sleep 200
end
```

Parallel execution:

```text
exec
  play "https://example.com/sounds", "success.mp3"
and
  repeat 5
    led_color ~ff0000, Led1
    sleep 500
  end
end
```

Sequential instructions are written on separate lines. An `exec` block finishes when its longest branch finishes.

## Workflow

1. Ask what emotion, duration, sound, lights, and ear movements are wanted.
2. Inspect existing choreographies and confirm supported syntax.
3. Find a suitably licensed sound when needed.
4. Require a stable direct MP3 or WAV URL returning HTTP 200.
5. Split a sound URL at its final slash:

```text
https://host/path/success.mp3
```

becomes:

```text
play "https://host/path", "success.mp3"
```

6. Calculate animation duration explicitly from every `sleep`.
7. Present the proposed experience and receive approval.
8. Open `/choregraphy/new`.
9. Fill in:
   - A descriptive title
   - A unique uppercase code
   - The choreography actions
10. Save without executing unless the user requested execution.

The OpenAPI currently exposes listing and playback, not creation, so create choreographies through the LiveView editor.

## Verification

After saving, verify all of the following:

- The UI reports `Choregraphy saved.`
- The editor navigates to `/choregraphy/<CODE>`
- `GET /api/choregraphies` contains the title and code
- The sound URL still returns HTTP 200 with an audio MIME type
- The calculated duration meets the request
- The final LED state is intentional

Only execute through the UI or:

```text
GET /api/choregraphies/<CODE>/play
```

when the user explicitly asks.

## Design Guidance

For entertaining animations:

- Use contrasting colors rather than tiny shade changes.
- Prefer rhythmic chases, pulses, flashes, or symmetrical patterns.
- Keep sleeps around 150–400 ms for energetic motion.
- Run sound and animation inside separate `exec` branches.
- End with an intentional pose: lights off, a signature color, or a calm resting state.
- Avoid rapid ear reversals that could stress the hardware.

## Common Mistakes

| Mistake | Correction |
|---|---|
| Using `#ff0000` | Use `~ff0000` |
| Passing one complete URL to `play` | Split it into root URL and filename |
| Using lowercase variable names | Variables begin with an uppercase letter |
| Assuming `LedAll` exists | Address `Led0` through `Led4` individually |
| Treating sleep values as seconds | Values are milliseconds |
| Inventing tracks or animation widgets | Write actions in the choreography DSL |
| Saving and immediately playing | Execute only with explicit permission |
| Leaving LEDs accidentally illuminated | Add an intentional final state |

## Completion Report

Report:

- Choreography title and code
- Estimated duration
- Sound source and license
- Visual behavior
- Verification evidence
- Whether it was executed
